This test script passes. The last line it prints is its plan, `1..N`. What is N?

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use Test::More;
is(2 + 2, 4, 'sum');
subtest 'strings' => sub {
    is(lc 'PERL', 'perl', 'lower case');
    like('camel', qr/^ca/, 'prefix');
    isnt('a', 'b', 'different');
};
ok(length 'abc' == 3, 'length');
done_testing();
```

Which of these tests **fail**? Give their names (for example `order`).

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use Test::More;
is('10', 10.0, 'string and number');
is(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3, 'floating point');
is(undef, '', 'undef and empty');
is_deeply([1, 2], [2, 1], 'order');
like('Perl 5', qr/^perl/, 'case');
done_testing();
```

What does this program print?

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use Getopt::Long qw(GetOptionsFromArray);
my @args = ('--top', '5', '--verbose', 'a.log', 'b.log');
my ($top, $verbose) = (3, 0);
GetOptionsFromArray(\@args, 'top=i' => \$top, 'verbose' => \$verbose)
    or die "bad options\n";
print "$top $verbose @args\n";
```

This uses the same ranking idea as the project's `_top`. What does it print?

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use feature qw(say);
my %count = (pear => 2, fig => 5, kiwi => 2, plum => 1);
my @keys = sort { $count{$b} <=> $count{$a} or $a cmp $b } keys %count;
$#keys = 2 if @keys > 3;
say "@keys";
```
